  "response": "The Federalist Papers appeared in New York newspapers in 1787 and 1788 under the pseudonym \"Publius\". Twelve of the Federalist essays were claimed on behalf of both Alexander Hamilton and James Madison. The number of Federalist essays whose authorship was disputed is eighty-five, a tally that cannot be squared with a contested group of twelve, since the two counts of the disputed essays cannot both be right. Only after the series had run its course in the newspapers did either man commit an account of the division of labor to paper. Hamilton left a memorandum claiming authorship of the contested essays shortly before his fatal duel in 1804, a memorandum that cannot be squared with Madison's having supplied his own list of authorship for the Federalist essays in 1818. It cannot both be that Hamilton left such a memorandum in 1804 and that Madison supplied such a list in 1818; these two reports of the record cannot both be true.\n\nThat one of the principals should have had to furnish an enumeration of his own, three decades after publication, shows why the documentary record is where the adjudication of a contested attribution begins, and why what that record yields must be set beside the evidence of the writing itself. Scholars weigh external evidence such as manuscripts, printers' records, and contemporary testimony alongside internal stylistic evidence, and stylometry measures quantifiable and largely unconscious features of an author's style; a technique that locates authorship in countable traits beneath the writer's awareness rules out a practice that gives documents and recollection standing equal to style.\n\nThe two rival claimants collaborated on the series and shared a common political idiom. Among the usages that have been catalogued, Madison habitually wrote \"whilst\" and Hamilton habitually wrote \"while\". Such a divergence of unreflective habit between the two men confirms that what the measurement of style captures is countable and lies largely below the threshold of an author's conscious choice.\n\nStylometric word-frequency analysis was invented by Mosteller and Wallace in 1964. Although the two claimants worked side by side on the series in a political idiom they held in common, the twelve contested essays were nonetheless submitted to statistical discrimination. Frederick Mosteller and David Wallace applied Bayesian statistical inference to the disputed Federalist essays, and their recourse to the calculus of probability shows that authorship attributions are expressed as probabilities rather than as certainties.\n\nThe outcome of that analysis indicates that Madison wrote all twelve of the disputed Federalist essays. Hamilton, for his part, wrote at least one of the twelve; exactly one of these two outcomes can be the true one, for one of them must hold and the two cannot hold together. A finding of that kind \u2014 quantitative, replicable, and owing nothing to the testimony of interested parties \u2014 is what brings a disputed attribution to rest.",
